{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head d5ab1e5a9e447bd4aedbc39d2a4b29948c49c186 on planner/client-note-custody-end-20261113, its one artifact `notes/2026-11-13-scope-client.md`, for request 43de81ee under promise 315e40ae and review request 576e01af. Section 5.5 closes the gap that NF-5 found, with no new phase, and it agrees with the C1 code at 5a2a6d82.\n\nWhat I read: the whole diff from main 324a0679, sections 5.3, 5.4, 5.5, 6 and 7 of the note at the head, and `packages/client/src/custody.ts` at the C1 head 5a2a6d82.\n\n- `absent` (`custody.ts:680-693`) already closes a dead latest signing and leaves a live one alone. C3 adds the outcome `unsent` and the phase `refused`, as section 13 says.\n- `unresolved()` (`custody.ts:776`) leaves out each phase in `TERMINAL` (`:112`), and `TERMINAL` holds `refused`. So a record that 5.5 ends leaves the 256, and case 1 of 5.5 (257 requests) holds.\n- `#asked` (`:581-585`) asks nothing for a `refused` record whose signings are all closed. After 5.5 only `folded` can find a late seal. `folded` (`:652-672`) skips only `FINAL` (`:286`, `accepted`, `archived` and `settled`), so a `refused` record moves to `accepted` when the intent digest matches a signing. The digest outlives the envelope, so case 2 holds.\n- `#resigns` (`:478-480`) signs again for a `refused` record and admits it against the capacity under the lease (`:505-506`). A member who submits again after 5.5 gets a new signing.\n- The section 7 schedule now covers a record that the client signed and never sent, which the old row missed. A read to the head after the death holds every entry that the scope sealed before that read, and a late seal goes through `folded`.\n\nWhat I checked: main 324a0679 is an ancestor, the head changes one path, and the artifact covers it at the head. The artifact is neither stale nor retired. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings, the commit-message check passes (1 checked, 0 flagged), and `git diff --check` is clean. I ran no tests, since the head changes no code.\n\nArchitecture: sound. `refused` already meant \"terminal for capacity and sending, open to proof of a seal\" (5.3). One more outcome, `unsent`, records that the client ended the record and at which head, and invents no scope refusal. Only a caller with a verified replica can end a record, so custody still never ends a record on a `not-found`.\n\nSecurity: no finding. `absent` takes the caller's word, and the caller is the client's own verified replica, as in 5.4. A wrong `absent` can only return a request to the member and free a slot of the 256. It can't make an entry or hide one, because the status of section 6 reads the replica.\n\nSimplification: no required change. Three notes for C3:\n\n1. The MCP service, the hosted agent and the CLI keep the gap. Their records with a dead latest signing still never end, so their stores can still fill at 256. The note states that choice. If the gap matters there, it needs its own request.\n2. The comment on `#close` (`custody.ts:600`) says the latest envelope stays. The code deletes `signing.envelope` for every index, and the latest envelope survives only because it lives in the record's top-level `envelope`. Keep that true when C3 writes 5.5.\n3. State what `send` returns for a record that ends with `unsent` when the caller passes neither `resign` nor `resubmit`. A resend of the dead bytes can only draw an expiry refusal.\n\nThis approval authorizes no landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:dfbcb2ba4bd96db677d416adc234cf8deba972ae","binding":"assigned","head":"d5ab1e5a9e447bd4aedbc39d2a4b29948c49c186","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2311ca5d9eeb9722e72ceab000da06b7b6ef4828\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:315e40ae5cc35b88494a1e56bdbc17485819efb0\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2311ca5d9eeb97